as I know that will afford you pleasure  that I have brought my odds taking to a  successful result I have determined to  write to you this letter to inform you  of everything that has been done and  discovered in this voyage of mine on the  33rd day after leaving Cadiz I came into  the Indian sea where I discovered many  islands inhabited by numerous peoples I  took possession of all of them for our  most fortunate King by making public  Proclamation on furling his standard no  one making any resistance to the first  of them I have given the name of our  blessed Savior trusting in whose aid  that I had reached this and all the rest  but the Indians called it Gua Nahanni to  each of the others I also gave a new  name ordering one to be called sancta  maria to concepción another Fernandina  another isabella and another johanna and  so with all the rest as soon as we  reached the island which i have just  said was called joanna i sailed along  its coast some considerable distance  towards the west and found it to be so  large without any apparent end that i  believed it was not an island but a but  a continent a province of Cathay but I  saw neither towns nor cities lying on  the seaboard only some villages and  country farms with whose inhabitants I  could not get speech because they fled  as soon as they beheld us I continued on  supposing I should come upon some city  or country houses at last finding that  no discoveries rewarded our further  progress and that this course was  leading us toward the north which I was  desirous of avoiding as it was winter in  these regions and it had always been my  intention to proceed southwards and the  winds were also favourable to such  desires I concluded not to attempt any  other adventures so turning back  I came again to a certain harbour which  I had remark  from the error sent two of our men into  the country to learn whether there was  any King or cities in that land they  journeyed for three days and found  innumerable people and habitations but  small and having no fixed government on  which account they returned meanwhile I  had learned from some Indians whom I had  seized at this place that the country  was was really an island consequently I  continued along towards the east as much  as 322 miles always hugging the shore  where was the very extremity of the  island from there I saw another Island  to the eastwards distant 54 miles from  Miss Joanna which I named Hispanic and  proceeded to it and directed my course  for five hundred sixty four miles east  by north as it were just as I had done  at Joanna the island called Joanna's as  well as the others in its neighborhood  is exceedingly fertile there's numerous  harbors and all sides very safe and wide  above comparison with anything I've ever  seen through it flow very many broad and  health-giving rivers and there any  numerous very lofty mountains all these  islands are very beautiful easy to be  traversed and full of the greatest  variety of trees reaching to the stars  I think these never lose their leaves as  I saw them looking as green and lovely  as they all want to be in the month of  May in Spain some of them were in leaf  and some in fruit each flourishing in  the condition its nature required the  nightingale was singing it in various  other little birds when I was rambling  among them in the month of November  they're also in the island called  Johanna seven or eight kinds of palms  which is readily surpassed ours in  height and beauty as to all the other  trees herbs and fruits  there are also wonderful pine woods  fields and extensive meadows birds of  various kinds and honey and all the  different metals they accept iron  in the island which I've said before was  called hispana there are many lofty and  beautiful mountains great farms groves  and fields most fertile both for  cultivation and for pasturage and well  adapted for constructing buildings the  convenience of the harbours in this  island and the excellent of the rivers  in volume and celebrity surpass human  belief unless one should see them in it  the trees pasture lands and fruits  differ much from those of Joanna besides  this is spanner abounds in various kinds  of spices gold and metals the  inhabitants of both sexes of this and  all of the other islands I have seen or  of which I have any knowledge always go  as naked as they came into this world  except that some of the women cover  their private parts with with leaves or  branches or or a veil of cotton which  they prepare themselves for this purpose  they are all as I said before unprovided  with any sort of iron and they are  destitute of arms which are entirely  unknown to them and for which they are  not adapted not on account of any bodily  deformity for they are well made but  because they are timid and full of  terror they carry however canes dried in  the Sun in place of weapons upon whose  roots they fix her wooden shaft dried  and sharpened to a point but they never  dare to make use of these for his often  happened when I'm when I've sent two or  three of my men to some of their  villages to speak with the inhabitants  the the crowd of Indians of sallied  forth but when they saw our men  approaching they speedily took flight  parents abandon their children and  children their parents this happened not  because of any loss or injury had been  inflicted on any of them are on the  contrary I gave whatever I had class and  many other things to whomsoever I  approach  with whom I could get speech without any  return being made to me but they are by  nature fearful and timid but when they  see that they are safe and all fear is  banished  they are very guileless and honest a  very liberal of all they have no one  refuses the Oscar anything that he  possesses are on the contrary they  themselves invite us to ask for it they  manifest the greatest affection towards  all of us exchanging valuable things for  trifles content with the very least  thing or nothing at all but i forbade  giving them very trifling thing and of  no value such as bits of plates dishes  and glass also nails and straps although  it seemed to them if they could get such  that they are quite the most beautiful  jewels in the world for chanced that a  sailor received for single strap as much  weight of gold as three gold Sallee D  and so others for things unless price  especially for new blankets of some gold  coins which they gave whatever the  seller asked for instance and ounce and  a half or two ounces of gold or thirty  or forty pounds of cotton with which  they are already familiar so to fit four  pieces are hoops jugs jars and pots they  bartered cotton and gold like beasts the  this i forbade because it was plainly  unjust and they gave them many beautiful  and pleasing things which I brought with  me for no return whatsoever in order to  win their affection and that they might  become Christians and inclined to love  out king and queen and princes and all  the people of Spain and that they might  be eager to search for and gather and  give to us what they are bound in and we  greatly need  they do not practice idolatry on the  contrary they believe that all strength  all power in short all blessings are  from heaven and that I've come down from  there with these ships and sailors and  in this spirit was I received everywhere  after they had got over their fear they  are neither lazy nor awkward but on the  contrary are of an excellent and acute  understanding those who have sailed  these seas get excellent accounts of  everything but they have never seen men  wearing clothes or more ships like ours  as soon as I had come into this sea I  took by force some Indians from the  first island in order that they might  learn from us and at the same time tell  us what they knew about affairs in these  regions  this succeeded admirably for in a short  time we understood them and they us both  by gesture and signs and words and they  were of great service to us they're  coming now with me and I've always  believed that I have come from heaven  notwithstanding a long time they have  been and still remain with us they were  the first who told this wherever we went  one calling to another with a loud voice  come come you will see men from heaven  upon both women and men children and  adults young and old laying aside the  fear they had felt before  [ __ ] eagerly to see us a great crowd  thronging about our steps some bringing  food and others drink with greatest love  an incredible goodwill in each Island  are many boats made of solid wood though  narrow yet in length and shaped similar  to our two bankers but swifter in motion  and managed by oars only some of them  are large some small and some of the  medium-sized but most are larger than a  - banker rowed by 18 oars with these  they sail to all of the islands which  are innumerable engaging in traffic and  commerce with each other I saw some of  these boats which carried 70 or 80  rowers in all these islands there is no  difference in the appearance of the  inhabitants so none of their customs and  language so that all understand one  another this is a circumstance most  favourable for what I believe our most  serene King especially desires that is  their conversion to the holy faith of  Christ which indeed as far as I can  understand they are ready and prune I've  told already how I sailed in a straight  course along the island of Johanna from  west to east 322 miles from this voyage  and the extent of my journeys I can say  that this Joe  is larger than England and Scotland  together for beyond the aforesaid 322  miles in that portion which looks  towards the West there are two more  provinces but which I did not visit one  of them the Indians called anon had its  inhabitants er born with tails these  provinces extend 180 miles as I learned  from the Indians whom I am bringing with  me and who are well-acquainted with all  these islands the distance around his  spanner is greater than all Spain from  Colonia to font Arabia as is readily  proved because its fourth side which I  myself traversed in a straight course  from West East stretched 540 miles this  island is to be coveted and not to be  despised to and acquired as I've already  taken possession of all the others as I  have said for almost invincible King and  the rule over them is entirely committed  to the said king so in this one I have  taken special possession over a certain  larger town in the most convenient spot  well-suited for all profit and commerce  to which I've given the name of the  Nativity of our Lord and there I ordered  a fort to be built forthwith which ought  to be finished now in it I left as many  men as seemed necessary with all kinds  of arms and provisions sufficient for  more than a year also a care avala and  men to build others skilled not only in  this trade but in others I support for  them the goodwill and remarkable  friendship of the king of the island for  these people are very affectionate and  kind so much so that the aforesaid King  took a pride in my being called his  brother  although shrewd they changed their minds  and wished to harm those who have  remained in the fort they cannot because  well they huh without arms go naked and  are too timid so that in truth those who  hold the aforesaid fort can lay waste to  the whole of the island without any  danger to themselves provided they do  not violate the rules and instructions I  have given them  in all these islands as I understand  every man is satisfied with only one  life except the princes or Kings who are  permitted to have 20 the women appear to  work more than the men but I could not  well understand whether they have  private property or not if I saw that  what everyone had was shared with the  others especially meals provisions in  men and such things I found among them  no monsters as many expected but men of  great deference and kind nor are they  black like the Ethiopians but they have  long straight hair they do not dwell  where the Rays of the sun's of most  power although the sun's heat is very  great there as this region is 26 degrees  distant from the equatorial line from  the summit's of the mountains there  comes great cold but the Indians  mitigated by being inured to the weather  and by the help of very hot food which  they consume frequently and in a  moderate quantities  I saw no monsters neither did I have  accounts of any such except in an island  called charas ii as one crosses over  from spain to india which is inhabited  by a certain race regarded by their  neighbors as very ferocious they eat  human flesh and make use of several  kinds of boats which they cross over to  all the indian islands and plunder and  carry off whatever they can but they  differ in no respect from the others  except in wearing their hair long after  the fashion of women they make use of  bows and arrows made of reeds having  pointed shafts fastened to the thicker  portion as we have before described for  this reason they are considered to be  ferocious and the other Indians  consequently are terribly afraid of them  they have intercourse with certain women  who dwell lone upon the island of  michurin the first as one crosses from  Spain to India these women follow none  of the usual occupations of their sex  but they use bows and arrows like those  of their husbands which I have described  and protect themselves with plates of  copper which is found in the greatest  abundance among them I was informed that  there is another island larger than the  aforesaid hispana whose inhabitants have  no hair and that there is greater  abundance of gold in it than in the  others some of the inhabitants of these  islands and of the others i have seen i  am bringing over with me to bear  testimony to what i've reported finally  to sum up in a few words the chief  results and advantage of our departure  and speedy return i make this promise to  almost invincible sovereign that if i am  supported by some little assistance from  them I will give them as much gold as  they have need of and in addition spices  cotton and mastic which is found only in  chios and as many heathen slaves as  their Majesty may choose to demand  besides these rhubarb and other kinds of  drugs which I think the men left in the  fort before alluded to have already  discovered or will do so as I myself  have delayed nowhere longer than the  winds compelled me except when I was  provided for the construction of a fort  in the city of Nativity and for making  all things safe although these matters  are very wonderful and unheard-of there  would have been more so if ships of a  reasonable account had been furnished me  board has been accomplished is great and  wonderful and not at all proportionate  to my desserts but to the sacred  Christian faith and to the piety and  religion of our sovereign for the mind  of a man could not compass the Spirit of  God has granted to mortals for God is  wont to listen to his servants who love  his precepts even in impossibilities as  has happened to me in the present  instance who has accomplished what human  strength has hit whom never attained for  if anyone has written or told anything  about these islands all have done so  either obscurely or by guesswork so this  has almost seemed to be fabulous  therefore let king and queen and princes  and their most fortunate realms and all  other Christian provinces that has all  returned thanks to our Lord and Savior  Jesus Christ who was bestowed so great a  victory and reward upon us  let there be processions and solemn  sacrifices prepared that the churches  bedecked with festal boughs that Christ  rejoice upon earth as he rejoices in  heaven as he foresees that so many souls  of so many people here to fall lost are  to be saved and let us be glad not only  for the exaltation of our faith but also  for the increase of temporal prosperity  in which not only Spain but all  Christendom is about to share as these  things have been accomplished so have  they been briefly narrated farewell  Christopher : Admiral of the ocean fleet  Lisbon  March 14th

